turns.text:
If the connection drops during a query, that query fails with a connection error. The catch block then tries to ROLLBACK, but the client is already disconnected, so that also fails—throwing a new connection error that masks the original one. This second error propagates up and the route handler sees a ROLLBACK error instead of knowing the write attempt failed.

The finally block still runs and releases the (now-dead) client.

The issue: if both the write and the ROLLBACK fail, the route handler gets the wrong error message. It also doesn't gracefully handle the fact that the server automatically rolled back the transaction when the connection dropped—calling ROLLBACK on a dead client is redundant and noisy.

The fix would be to ignore ROLLBACK failures caused by disconnection:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // Connection already closed; transaction is rolled back server-side
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error and lets the route handler see what actually happened.

